 LF blob'\"\n+    ],\n+    \"actual_exit\": 1,\n+    \"stdout_sha256\": \"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\",\n+    \"stderr_sha256\": \"6aa137a0decb19d1bd2a502c2cbb414eb49363ea1f27bc75479b4caf4f159b80\"\n+  },\n+  \"pre_RAW_sha256\": \"5515f2800132a23830d92bace395942e1546d1446870a956c5e3c0513b2007f0\",\n+  \"staged_and_post_RAW_sha256\": \"88745d354bbc89880245ca3c5be78a79f383412d27712064024d0a5ade4cf414\",\n+  \"CRLF_count_before\": 15550,\n+  \"correction\": \"Product MANIFEST.md only: exact CRLF-to-LF conversion and scoped eol=lf attribute. All textual content and the approved appended paragraph unchanged; exact before/after full bytes retained. No source/header/metadata changes. Prior integration receipt remains historical pre-normalization evidence.\",\n+  \"failure_class\": \"IMPLEMENTATION_FAILED\",\n+  \"salvage\": \"All original bytes retained; no mathematical route or fragment discarded.\"\n+}"},{"sha":"e4bcc0292f9c8358b10d45b0e735db38c3f3d490","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/source-141-text.txt","status":"added","additions":55,"deletions":0,"changes":55,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-141-text.txt","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-141-text.txt","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-141-text.txt?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@\n+7.12 Optimistic Follow-the-Regularized-Leader129\n+using the prediction strategy in Algorithm 7.7, for allu∈R d, we have\n+TX\n+t=1\n+1\n+2(⟨zt,x t⟩ −y t)2 −\n+TX\n+t=1\n+1\n+2(⟨zt,u⟩ −y t)2 ≤ λ\n+2 ∥u∥2\n+2 + dY 2\n+2 ln\n+^R\n+1 + R2T\n+λd\n+^S\n+.\n+Remark 7.45It is possible to show that the regret of the Vovk–Azoury–Warmuth forecaster\n+is optimal up to multiplicative factors (Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi, 2006, Theorem 11.9).\n+Remark 7.46We say that an online learning algorithm isimproperif the prediction\n+strategy can be chosen from a larger class than the competitor one. So, the Vovk–Azoury–\n+Warmuth forecaster is improper because we measure its regret with respect to a linear pre-\n+dictoru, but the Vovk–Azoury–Warmuth prediction depends in a non-linear way onz t.\n+7.12 Optimistic Follow-the-Regularized-Leader\n+Algorithm 7.8Optimistic Follow-the-Regularized-Leader (Optimistic FTRL)\n+Require:A sequence of regularizersψ 1, . . . , ψT :X →R, closed non-empty convex set\n+V ⊆ X ⊆R d\n+1:fort= 1toTdo\n+2:Predict next loss ˜ℓt :V →R\n+3:Outputx t ∈argmin x∈V ψt(x) + ˜ℓt(x) +Pt−1\n+i=1 ℓi(x)\n+4:Receiveℓ t :R d →(−∞,+∞]such thatx t ∈domℓ t, and pay the lossℓ t(xt)\n+5:end for\n+In Section 6.11, we have seen that it is possible to achieve better regret guarantees through\n+the so-called Optimistic OMD, which uses a prediction of the next gradient. Here, we will\n+extend the same idea to FTRL, where we predict the next loss function. If our predicted\n+loss is correct, we can expect the regret to decrease. However, if our prediction is wrong,\n+we still want to recover the worst-case guarantee. Such an algorithm is calledOptimistic\n+FTRL, see Algorithm 7.8. As in Optimistic OMD, we do not make any assumption on how\n+the prediction of the next loss is generated.\n+Let’s see why this is a good idea. Remember that FTRL simply predicts with the mini-\n+mizer of the previous losses plus a time-varying regularizer. Let’s assume for a moment that\n+instead we have the gift of predicting the future, so we do know the next loss ahead of time.\n+Then, we could predict with its minimizer and suffer a negative regret. However, probably\n+our foresight abilities are not so powerful, so our prediction of the next loss might be in-\n+accurate. In this case, a better idea might be just to add our predicted loss to the previous\n+ones and minimize the regularized sum. We would expect the regret guarantee to improve if\n+our prediction of the future loss is precise. At the same time, if the prediction is wrong, we\n+expect its influence to be limited, given that we use it together with all the past losses.\n+All these intuitions can be formalized in the following theorem.\n+Theorem 7.47With the notation in Algorithm 7.8, letVbe convex, closed, non-empty. De-\n+note byF t(x) =ψ t(x) +Pt−1\n+i=1 ℓi(x). Fort= 1, . . . , T, ifF t + ˜ℓt is closed, subdifferen-\n+tiable, and strongly convex inV, thenx t exists and is unique. In addition, fort= 1, . . . , T,\n\\ No newline at end of file"},{"sha":"07b23211018bf11148d7e303b6d970d1aeb88769","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/source-141.png","status":"added","additions":0,"deletions":0,"changes":0,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-141.png","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-141.png","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-141.png?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6"},{"sha":"7069ab2b82d726562b5e9d27d4a18420da1d6cd3","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/source-142-text.txt","status":"added","additions":71,"deletions":0,"changes":71,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-142-text.txt","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-142-text.txt","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-142-text.txt?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,71 @@\n+130Follow-the-Regularized-Leader\n+assume∂(ℓ t − ˜ℓt)(xt)to be non-empty andF t +ℓ t to be closed, subdifferentiable, and\n+λt-strongly convex with respect to∥ · ∥inV. Then, we have\n+TX\n+t=1\n+(ℓt(xt)−ℓ t(u))≤ψ T (u)−ψ 1(x1) +\n+TX\n+t=1\n+^T\n+⟨ˆgt,x t −x t+1⟩ − λt\n+2 ∥xt −x t+1∥2\n+^U\n++\n+T−1X\n+t=1\n+[ψt(xt+1)−ψ t+1(xt+1)]\n+≤ψ T (u)−ψ 1(x1) +\n+TX\n+t=1\n+∥ˆgt∥2\n+⋆\n+2λt\n++\n+T−1X\n+t=1\n+[ψt(xt+1)−ψ t+1(xt+1)],\n+for allu∈ Vand all ˆgt ∈∂(ℓ t − ˜ℓt)(xt)fort= 1, . . . , T.\n+ProofWe can interpret the Optimistic FTRL as FTRL with a regularizer ˜ψt(x) =ψ t(x)+\n+˜ℓt(x). So, as in Lemma 7.5, the existence and uniqueness ofx t is given by Theorem 6.9.\n+Also, note that ˜ℓT+1 does not influence the regret of the algorithm over theTrounds, so we\n+can set ˜ℓT+1 = ˜ℓT .\n+DefineF t(x) =ψ t(x) +Pt−1\n+i=1 ℓi(x). We can use Lemma 7.1 withψ T+1 =ψ T . Given\n+thatu∈ V, we can discard the last term in the lemma because non-positive, to obtain\n+TX\n+t=1\n+ℓt(xt)−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+ℓt(u)\n+≤ ˜ℓT (u) +ψ T (u)−min\n+x∈V\n+(˜ℓ1(x) +ψ 1(x))\n++\n+TX\n+t=1\n+[Ft(xt)−F t+1(xt+1) +ℓ t(xt)] +\n+T−1X\n+t=1\n+[˜ℓt(xt)− ˜ℓt+1(xt+1)]\n+=ψ T (u)−ψ 1(x1) +\n+TX\n+t=1\n+[Ft(xt)−F t+1(xt+1) +ℓ t(xt)],\n+where we used the fact that the terms ˜ℓt(xt)− ˜ℓt+1(xt+1)form a telescopic sum and\n+minx∈V (˜ℓ1(x)+ψ1(x)) = ˜ℓ1(x1)+ψ1(x1). Now focus on the termsFt(xt)−Ft+1(xt+1)+\n+ℓt(xt). Observe thatF t(x)+ℓ t(x)+ι V(x)isλ t-strongly convex with respect to∥·∥, hence\n+from Lemma 4.2 we have\n+Ft(xt)−F t+1(xt+1) +ℓ t(xt)\n+= (Ft(xt) +ℓ t(xt))−(F t(xt+1) +ℓ t(xt+1)) +ψ t(xt+1)−ψ t+1(xt+1)\n+≤ ⟨ˆgt,x t −x t+1⟩ − λt\n+2 ∥xt −x t+1∥2 +ψ t(xt+1)−ψ t+1(xt+1),\n+for all ˆgt ∈∂(F t +ℓ t +ι V)(xt). Observing thatx t = argminx∈V Ft(x)+ ˜ℓt(x), we have\n+0∈∂(F t+˜ℓt+ιV)(xt). Hence, using Theorem 2.23,∂(ℓt−˜ℓt)(xt)⊆∂(F t+ℓt+ιV)(xt).\n+For the second inequality, by the definition of dual norms, we have that\n+⟨ˆgt,x t−xt+1⟩− λt∥xt −x t+1∥2\n+2 ≤ ∥ˆgt∥⋆∥xt−xt+1∥− λt∥xt −x t+1∥2\n+2 ≤ ∥ˆgt∥2\n+⋆\n+2λt\n+.\n\\ No newline at end of file"},{"sha":"f595c0857c3f82936b17e6a64b75590440f58fee","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/source-142.png","status":"added","additions":0,"deletions":0,"changes":0,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-142.png","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-142.png","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-142.png?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6"},{"sha":"3867458c928abd0302e0e8edb2cf1b04345d47c1","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/source-278-text.txt","status":"added","additions":93,"deletions":0,"changes":93,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-278-text.txt","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-278-text.txt","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-278-text.txt?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,93 @@\n+266Saddle-Point Optimization and Online Algorithms\n+Algorithm 15.9Be-the-Regularized-Leader Algorithm\n+Require:Non-empty closed convexV ⊂ X ⊆R d,ψ 1, . . . , ψT regularizers\n+1:fort= 1toTdo\n+2:Receiveℓ t :R d →(−∞,+∞], whereV ⊆domℓ t\n+3:x t ∈argmin x∈V ψt(x) +Pt\n+i=1 ℓi(x)\n+4:Pay the lossℓ t(xt)\n+5:end for\n+where in the last equality we used Lemma 6.7. Dividing byη t and summing overt=\n+1, . . . , T, we have\n+TX\n+t=1\n+(ℓt(xt)−ℓ t(u))≤\n+TX\n+t=1\n+^R Bψ(u;x t−1)\n+ηt\n+− Bψ(u;x t)\n+ηt\n+^S\n+−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+Bψ(xt;x t−1)\n+ηt\n+= Bψ(u;x 0)\n+η1\n+− Bψ(u;x T )\n+ηT\n++\n+T−1X\n+t=1\n+^R 1\n+ηt+1\n+− 1\n+ηt\n+^S\n+Bψ(u;x t)−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+Bψ(xt;x t−1)\n+ηt\n+≤ D2\n+η1\n++D 2\n+T−1X\n+t=1\n+^R 1\n+ηt+1\n+− 1\n+ηt\n+^S\n+−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+Bψ(xt;x t−1)\n+ηt\n+= D2\n+η1\n++D 2\n+^R 1\n+ηT\n+− 1\n+η1\n+^S\n+−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+Bψ(xt;x t−1)\n+ηt\n+= D2\n+ηT\n+−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+Bψ(xt;x t−1)\n+ηt\n+,\n+where we denoted byD 2 = max0≤t≤T−1 Bψ(u;x t).\n+The second statement is left as an exercise.\n+The regret of Prescient Online Mirror Descent contains the negative terms we needed\n+from the optimistic algorithms.\n+Analogously, we can obtain a version of FTRL that uses the knowledge of the current\n+loss:Be-The-Regularized-Leader (BTRL), that predicts in each time step withx t ∈\n+argminx∈V ψt(x) +Pt\n+i=1 ℓi(x), see Algorithm 15.9.\n+Ifψ t ≡0, then Be-The-Regularized-Leader becomes theBe-The-Leaderalgorithm.\n+BTRL can be thought of as Optimistic FTRL where ˜ℓt =ℓ t. Hence, from the regret of\n+Optimistic FTRL in Theorem 7.47, we immediately have the following theorem.\n+Theorem 15.31LetV ⊂R d be convex, closed, and non-empty. Assume fort= 1, . . . , T\n+thatψ t +Pt\n+i=1 ℓi is proper, closed, andλ t-strongly convex with respect to∥ · ∥. Then, for\n\\ No newline at end of file"},{"sha":"01d86b84c43ed21e6ac87507cd3da4ff7868e2e3","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/source-279-text.txt","status":"added","additions":73,"deletions":0,"changes":73,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-279-text.txt","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-279-text.txt","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-279-text.txt?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@\n+15.6 History Bits267\n+allu∈ V, Algorithm 15.9 satisfies\n+TX\n+t=1\n+ℓt(xt)−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+ℓt(u)\n+≤ψ T (u)−ψ 1(x1)−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+λt\n+2 ∥xt −x t+1∥2 +\n+T−1X\n+t=1\n+(ψt(xt+1)−ψ t+1(xt+1)).\n+Remark 15.32In the Be-The-Leader algorithm, if all theψ t ≡0, then the theorem states\n+that the regret is non-positive.\n+Notably, the non-negative gradient terms are missing in the bound of BTRL, but we still\n+have the negative ones associated with the change inx t.\n+Using, for example, BTRL for theX-player and Optimistic FTRL for theY-player, we\n+have\n+TX\n+t=1\n+f(x t,y)−\n+TX\n+t=1\n+f(x,y t)≤ψ X (x) +ψ Y(y) + ∥gY,1∥2\n+Y,⋆\n+λY\n++\n+TX\n+t=2\n+^R^R2L2\n+X Y\n+λY\n+− λX\n+4\n+^S\n+∥xt −x t−1∥2\n+X +\n+^R2L2\n+YY\n+λY\n+− λY\n+4\n+^S\n+∥yt −y t−1∥2\n+Y\n+^S\n+.\n+15.6 History Bits\n+Theorem 15.3 is Rockafellar (1970, Lemma 36.2). The proof of Theorem 15.12 is from Liu\n+and Orabona (2022) and, in turn, is based on the one in Abernethy and Wang (2017): Liu and\n+Orabona (2022) stressed the dependence of the regret on a competitor that can be useful for\n+refined bounds. Different variants of this theorem are known in the game theory community\n+as “Folk Theorems”, because such a result was widely known among game theorists in the\n+1950s, even though no one had published it.\n+The celebrated minimax theorem for zero-sum two-person games was first discovered by\n+John von Neumann in the 1920s (Neumann, 1928; Neumann and Morgenstern, 1944). The\n+version stated here is a simplification of the generalization due to Sion (1958). The proof\n+here is from Abernethy and Wang (2017). A similar proof is in Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi\n+(2006) based on a discretization of the space that in turn is based on the one in Freund and\n+Schapire (1996, 1999b).\n+Algorithms 15.2 and 15.3 are a generalization of the algorithm for boosting in Freund and\n+Schapire (1996, 1999b). Algorithm 15.2 was also used in Abernethy and Wang (2017) to\n+recover variants of the Frank–Wolfe algorithm (Frank and Wolfe, 1956).\n+It is not clear who invented alternation: it was a known trick used in implementations of\n+CFR for the computer poker competition from 2010 or so 1. Note that in CFR, the method\n+of choice is Regret Matching (Hart and Mas-Colell, 2000). However, Kroer (2020) em-\n+pirically shows that alternation improves even OGD for solving bilinear games. Tammelin\n+et al. (2015) explicitly include this trick in their implementation of an improved version of\n+1 Christian Kroer, 2021, personal communication.\n\\ No newline at end of file"},{"sha":"5e0b5a498828c11d4861de3d5ef69c587b904877","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/source-279.png","status":"added","additions":0,"deletions":0,"changes":0,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-279.png","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-279.png","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fsource-279.png?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6"},{"sha":"cc4fa698032840a2d7778d2b655b8c8f9863103b","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/v1.json","status":"added","additions":235,"deletions":0,"changes":235,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fv1.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fv1.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fv1.json?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,235 @@\n+{\n+  \"status\": \"read-only readiness proposal; all mandatory targets OPEN\",\n+  \"actor\": \"/root/adaptive_decoder\",\n+  \"requested_model\": \"GPT-6 Astra\",\n+  \"requested_effort\": \"medium\",\n+  \"runtime_attestation\": false,\n+  \"input_raw_sha256\": {\n+    \"docs/contracts/online-ch2-complete-target-freeze-v1/effective-targets-frozen-v3.2.json\": \"61cf1901a632aeee35ee71511ba53b115ae0958649ea78b786536a4404684ba1\",\n+    \"tmp/online-ch2-forward-source-repair-20261011/complete-freeze-proposal-v2.json\": \"93cbf19b28181091a4472276e8e8bf04a4fcc6547eaf238c4da4ae5e102696e0\",\n+    \"runs/online-ch2-weighted-identity-20261011/accepted-bounded-package-v1.json\": \"d9d9ce4bcbc4e07fe984d32348d296966b173bfa8d3f519ff5a77f77ebecb14c\",\n+    \"runs/online-ch2-prescient-source-20261010/FINAL-review-v1.json\": \"651f566002add20400c62aacb5e6b84760358c127e3281f684e790643a53d00f\",\n+    \"BanditRLProof/OnlineWeightedPotential.lean\": \"63d94480e5e5a337acf5019c62e092fd36b1d2bf1a6edfc6f5b6bc5b95aa885a\",\n+    \"BanditRLProof/OnlinePrescientBregmanSource.lean\": \"57832c9dfb4e915f9a0578ffb122792b0aac2b3404da4f6c00f2aa9fcd36bc02\",\n+    \"BanditRLProof/OnlineFTLSelector.lean\": \"cd1cae5683edaa40fde18466f1efbca8ad34b295d8dfc3b173a35e0d459405df\",\n+    \"BanditRLProof/FTRLOneStep.lean\": \"abbf512c22136ebf713e035faf5bff2debce238582219d469e34bbb7dcb98d09\",\n+    \"BanditRLProof/OnlineProximalComparison.lean\": \"04dae0024d9a2b61016b4652767f4d678725325a040a02c62680a202ec2593d2\",\n+    \".lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Analysis/Convex/Strong.lean\": \"6ff478d50f5adf0d215c8745530645f1d2c041308752df5c0cc47f302f6f2b42\",\n+    \"BanditRLProof/TsallisFTRLMinimizerExistence.lean\": \"4736a208baff9b0e895fda667fe5f5570624bbf7ba4526aaa6c8268c468227c4\"\n+  },\n+  \"exact_frozen_target_rows\": [\n+    {\n+      \"target_id\": \"repair:7.47-terminal\",\n+      \"source_id\": \"forward:prescient-lookahead\",\n+      \"scope_tag\": \"necessarydependency\",\n+      \"domain\": \"Finite-dimensional real Euclidean space unless explicitly quantified otherwise\",\n+      \"assumptions\": \"Exactly the explicit premises in effective_statement and named child interfaces; no historical recommendation adds premises implicitly\",\n+      \"effective_statement\": \"For G_t=psi_t+sum_(i<=t)ell_i,arbitrary feasible z=x_(T+1) and finite displayed values, R_T(u)=psi_T(u)-psi1(x1)+sum_(t<=T)(G_t(x_t)-G_t(x_(t+1)))+sum_(t<T)(psi_t(x_(t+1))-psi_(t+1)(x_(t+1)))+G_T(z)-G_T(u). Mandatory labelled correction: retain real-round Algorithm7.8/hypotheses; define ghost psi_(T+1)=psi_T,h_(T+1)=0 and z in argmin G_T, proving attainment and all support/finiteness conditions. Then terminal gap<=0 yields printed sharp bound and Young bound. Repeating final hint instead requires adding h_(T+1)(u)-h_(T+1)(z), not cancelling it. The repeated-hint quadratic completion is refuted; no statement of equivalence to literal original is accepted.\",\n+      \"branch\": \"terminal statement correction\",\n+      \"status\": \"mandatory source-repair contract open\",\n+      \"implementation_acceptance\": false,\n+      \"semantic_decisions_pending\": [\n+        \"B4\"\n+      ],\n+      \"proof_body_ready\": false,\n+      \"proof_body_ready_reason\": \"No next proof before distinct effective-target freeze approval; listed semantic decisions and open repairs remain explicit.\",\n+      \"claim_kind\": \"mathematical target or required repair obligation\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"target_id\": \"repair:15.31-terminal\",\n+      \"source_id\": \"forward:prescient-lookahead\",\n+      \"scope_tag\": \"necessarydependency\",\n+      \"domain\": \"Finite-dimensional real Euclidean space unless explicitly quantified otherwise\",\n+      \"assumptions\": \"Exactly the explicit premises in effective_statement and named child interfaces; no historical recommendation adds premises implicitly\",\n+      \"effective_statement\": \"For BTRL with T>=1,proper closed lambda_t-strongly convex cumulative G_t,lambda_t>0,finite feasible played/comparator values and actual minimizers x_t, define ghost x_(T+1)=x_T. Prove R_T(u)<=psi_T(u)-psi1(x1)-sum_(t<T)lambda_t||x_t-x_(t+1)||^2/2+sum_(t<T)(psi_t(x_(t+1))-psi_(t+1)(x_(t+1))). Optional stronger endpoint is-lambda_T||u-x_T||^2/2. Original Algorithm15.9 defines no x_(T+1); repeating last loss gives checked0<=-1/36 and is not accepted.\",\n+      \"branch\": \"terminal statement correction\",\n+      \"status\": \"mandatory source-repair contract open\",\n+      \"implementation_acceptance\": false,\n+      \"semantic_decisions_pending\": [\n+        \"B4\"\n+      ],\n+      \"proof_body_ready\": false,\n+      \"proof_body_ready_reason\": \"No next proof before distinct effective-target freeze approval; listed semantic decisions and open repairs remain explicit.\",\n+      \"claim_kind\": \"mathematical target or required repair obligation\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"target_id\": \"dependency:Algorithm7.8-Theorem7.47\",\n+      \"source_id\": \"Algorithm7.8-Theorem7.47\",\n+      \"scope_tag\": \"necessarydependency\",\n+      \"domain\": \"Finite-dimensional real Euclidean space unless explicitly quantified otherwise\",\n+      \"assumptions\": \"Exactly the explicit premises in effective_statement and named child interfaces; no historical recommendation adds premises implicitly\",\n+      \"effective_statement\": \"Effective target is repair:7.47-terminal; immutable original algorithm/statement/hypotheses are retained in archival_sources.\",\n+      \"branch\": \"selected dependency\",\n+      \"status\": \"terminal source-statement repair open\",\n+      \"implementation_acceptance\": false,\n+      \"source_locator\": {\n+        \"pdf_pages_1based\": [\n+          141,\n+          142\n+        ],\n+        \"printed_pages\": [\n+          129,\n+          130\n+        ],\n+        \"marker\": \"Algorithm 7.8\",\n+        \"excerpt\": \"Algorithm 7.8Optimistic Follow-the-Regularized-Leader (Optimistic FTRL)\\nRequire:A sequence of regularizersψ 1, . . . , ψT :X →R, closed non-empty convex set\\nV ⊆ X ⊆Rd\\n1:fort= 1toTdo\\n2:Predict next loss ˜ℓt :V →R\\n3:Outputx t ∈argmin x∈V ψt(x) + ˜ℓt(x) + Pt−1\\ni=1 ℓi(x)\\n4:Receiveℓ t :R d →(−∞,+∞]such thatx t ∈domℓ t, and pay the lossℓ t(xt)\\n5:end for\\nIn Section 6.11, we have seen that it is possible to achieve better regret guarantees through\\nthe so-called Optimistic OMD, which uses a prediction of the next gradient. Here, we will\\nextend the same idea to FTRL, where we predict the next loss function. If our predicted\\nloss is correct, we can expect the regret to decrease. However, if our prediction is wrong,\\nwe still want to recover the worst-case guarantee. Such an algorithm is calledOptimistic\\nFTRL, see Algorithm 7.8. As in Optimistic OMD, we do not make any assumption on how\\nthe prediction of the next loss is generated.\\nLet’s see why this is a good idea. Remember that FTRL simply predicts with the mini-\\nmizer of the previous losses plus a time-varying regularizer. Let’s assume for a m\",\n+        \"excerpt_sha256\": \"d76b4a8cdcb465d7f6bd1d00a7bbd95a09c51819b85e55402f4c89f2ab8107ae\"\n+      },\n+      \"semantic_decisions_pending\": [],\n+      \"proof_body_ready\": false,\n+      \"proof_body_ready_reason\": \"No next proof before distinct effective-target freeze approval; listed semantic decisions and open repairs remain explicit.\",\n+      \"claim_kind\": \"mathematical target or required repair obligation\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"target_id\": \"dependency:Algorithm15.9-Theorem15.31\",\n+      \"source_id\": \"Algorithm15.9-Theorem15.31\",\n+      \"scope_tag\": \"optionalroute\",\n+      \"domain\": \"Finite-dimensional real Euclidean space unless explicitly quantified otherwise\",\n+      \"assumptions\": \"Exactly the explicit premises in effective_statement and named child interfaces; no historical recommendation adds premises implicitly\",\n+      \"effective_statement\": \"Effective target is repair:15.31-terminal; original retained and never silently omitted.\",\n+      \"branch\": \"selected dependency\",\n+      \"status\": \"route optional for C2 implication, retained theorem repair remains required/open\",\n+      \"implementation_acceptance\": false,\n+      \"source_locator\": {\n+        \"pdf_pages_1based\": [\n+          278,\n+          279\n+        ],\n+        \"printed_pages\": [\n+          266,\n+          267\n+        ],\n+        \"marker\": \"Algorithm 15.9\",\n+        \"excerpt\": \"Algorithm 15.9Be-the-Regularized-Leader Algorithm\\nRequire:Non-empty closed convexV ⊂ X ⊆Rd,ψ 1, . . . , ψT regularizers\\n1:fort= 1toTdo\\n2:Receiveℓ t :R d →(−∞,+∞], whereV ⊆domℓ t\\n3:x t ∈argmin x∈V ψt(x) + Pt\\ni=1 ℓi(x)\\n4:Pay the lossℓ t(xt)\\n5:end for\\nwhere in the last equality we used Lemma 6.7. Dividing byη t and summing overt=\\n1, . . . , T, we have\\nTX\\nt=1\\n(ℓt(xt)−ℓ t(u))≤\\nTX\\nt=1\\n\\^RBψ(u;xt−1)\\nηt\\n− Bψ(u;xt)\\nηt\\n\\^S\\n−\\nTX\\nt=1\\nBψ(xt;xt−1)\\nηt\\n= Bψ(u;x0)\\nη1\\n− Bψ(u;xT )\\nηT\\n+\\nT−1X\\nt=1\\n\\^R 1\\nηt+1\\n− 1\\nηt\\n\\^S\\nBψ(u;xt)−\\nTX\\nt=1\\nBψ(xt;xt−1)\\nηt\\n≤ D2\\nη1\\n+D 2\\nT−1X\\nt=1\\n\\^R 1\\nηt+1\\n− 1\\nηt\\n\\^S\\n−\\nTX\\nt=1\\nBψ(xt;xt−1)\\nηt\\n= D2\\nη1\\n+D 2\\n\\^R 1\\nηT\\n− 1\\nη1\\n\\^S\\n−\\nTX\\nt=1\\nBψ(xt;xt−1)\\nηt\\n= D2\\nηT\\n−\\nTX\\nt=1\\nBψ(xt;xt−1)\\nηt\\n,\\nwhere we denoted byD 2 = max0≤t≤T−1 Bψ(u;xt).\\nThe second statement is left as an exercise.\\nThe regret of Prescient Online Mirror Descent contains the negative terms we needed\\nfrom the optimistic algorithms.\\nAnalogously, we can obtain a version of FTRL that uses the knowledge of the current\\nloss:Be-The-Regularized-Leader (BTRL), that predicts in each time step withx t ∈\\nargminx∈V ψt(x) + Pt\\ni=1 ℓi(x), see Algorithm 15.9\",\n+        \"excerpt_sha256\": \"8959c97909101e663ecee57b13505c91f6a0ad917eac09dc05452b9537e950aa\"\n+      },\n+      \"semantic_decisions_pending\": [],\n+      \"proof_body_ready\": false,\n+      \"proof_body_ready_reason\": \"No next proof before distinct effective-target freeze approval; listed semantic decisions and open repairs remain explicit.\",\n+      \"claim_kind\": \"mathematical target or required repair obligation\",\n+      \"scope\": {\n+        \"primary_chapter2_euclidean_implication\": \"optionalroute\",\n+        \"book_goal_required\": true,\n+        \"required_in_owning_chapter\": 15,\n+        \"scope_basis\": \"Retained source mathematics or mandatory source repair in the owning chapter; optional only as a route for the primary Chapter2 Euclidean implication. Existing source/repair distinction unchanged.\"\n+      }\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \"accepted_substrate_joins\": [\n+    {\n+      \"module\": \"BanditRLProof/OnlineWeightedPotential.lean\",\n+      \"declarations\": [\n+        \"BanditRL.OnlineWeightedPotential.weighted_difference_sum_eq\"\n+      ],\n+      \"accepted_sha256\": \"63d94480e5e5a337acf5019c62e092fd36b1d2bf1a6edfc6f5b6bc5b95aa885a\",\n+      \"current_raw_sha256\": \"63d94480e5e5a337acf5019c62e092fd36b1d2bf1a6edfc6f5b6bc5b95aa885a\",\n+      \"HEAD_blob_sha256\": \"63d94480e5e5a337acf5019c62e092fd36b1d2bf1a6edfc6f5b6bc5b95aa885a\",\n+      \"exact_join\": true,\n+      \"reuse_boundary\": \"Accepted finite real sum algebra; optional reuse for telescope, not the B4 identity itself.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"module\": \"BanditRLProof/OnlinePrescientBregmanSource.lean\",\n+      \"declarations\": [\n+        \"BanditRL.OnlineConvex.sourceProper_of_domain\",\n+        \"BanditRL.OnlinePrescientBregman.advance_eq_some_of_minimizer\"\n+      ],\n+      \"accepted_sha256\": \"57832c9dfb4e915f9a0578ffb122792b0aac2b3404da4f6c00f2aa9fcd36bc02\",\n+      \"current_raw_sha256\": \"57832c9dfb4e915f9a0578ffb122792b0aac2b3404da4f6c00f2aa9fcd36bc02\",\n+      \"HEAD_blob_sha256\": \"57832c9dfb4e915f9a0578ffb122792b0aac2b3404da4f6c00f2aa9fcd36bc02\",\n+      \"exact_join\": true,\n+      \"reuse_boundary\": \"Accepted properness/minimum-identity patterns; source-specific proximal selector is not OptimisticFTRL.\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \"source_pdf\": {\n+    \"path\": \"E:\\\\ABRL\\\\worktrees\\\\research-online-ogd\\\\tmp\\\\pdfs\\\\orabona-v10.pdf\",\n+    \"sha256\": \"cef4edfa97a6e063e53e9c532717c50aa156e5bc782ea49f969b3385011a1b17\",\n+    \"pages\": [\n+      {\n+        \"pdf_page\": 141,\n+        \"printed_page\": 129,\n+        \"text_sha256\": \"5b37c2758f3bccd46312bcdb40a997b714eb849080b7a203ae99454c9dc583ad\",\n+        \"text_path\": \"source-141-text.txt\",\n+        \"pixel_personally_inspected\": true,\n+        \"pixel_sha256\": \"eacd665a728fa4bad76135f73257caea8cf20a96994126425f55f467aca50778\"\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \"pdf_page\": 142,\n+        \"printed_page\": 130,\n+        \"text_sha256\": \"8547ad7988a4dbb1b5f080bcfee0e008bf745b988704cdef83d98491229f53b9\",\n+        \"text_path\": \"source-142-text.txt\",\n+        \"pixel_personally_inspected\": true,\n+        \"pixel_sha256\": \"26a4994bf093a5166eb1a1680cbc1be9e68e415b3eadf7933c78c962e56034ea\"\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \"pdf_page\": 278,\n+        \"printed_page\": 266,\n+        \"text_sha256\": \"81a9b2322b61cb62cba6628798e81d3e99107057dda69e5a7b16d07d34304630\",\n+        \"text_path\": \"source-278-text.txt\",\n+        \"pixel_personally_inspected\": false,\n+        \"pixel_sha256\": null\n+      },\n+      {\n+        \"pdf_page\": 279,\n+        \"printed_page\": 267,\n+        \"text_sha256\": \"b44a96f194dce5812ae9d4b97b0dd0ed9946f900fe1556ecc35adbb627328b88\",\n+        \"text_path\": \"source-279-text.txt\",\n+        \"pixel_personally_inspected\": true,\n+        \"pixel_sha256\": \"08293ec9ebf9839584c2ddb694116fd142d7b1437ff11bc135896863bc11d3d8\"\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  },\n+  \"prior_counterexample\": {\n+    \"id\": \"terminal-repeat-loss-T1\",\n+    \"pages\": [\n+      142,\n+      278,\n+      279\n+    ],\n+    \"scope\": \"conditional counterexample to printed bound with the source7.47 repeat-prediction terminal convention transported to BTRL; not a refutation of every possible terminal repair\",\n+    \"T\": 1,\n+    \"V\": \"R\",\n+    \"psi1\": \"x^2/2\",\n+    \"loss1\": \"(x-1)^2/2\",\n+    \"predicted_loss1\": \"same as loss1\",\n+    \"terminal_convention\": \"psi2=psi1, predicted_loss2=predicted_loss1=loss1; x2 minimizes psi1+2loss1\",\n+    \"x1\": \"1/2\",\n+    \"x2\": \"2/3\",\n+    \"u\": \"1/2\",\n+    \"lambda1\": \"2\",\n+    \"stationarity_checks\": {\n+      \"initial\": \"2*(1/2)-1=0\",\n+      \"terminal\": \"3*(2/3)-2=0\"\n+    },\n+    \"lhs_regret\": \"0\",\n+    \"rhs_printed_sharp_bound\": \"-1/36\",\n+    \"contradiction\": \"0 <= -1/36 is false\",\n+    \"candidate_repair_not_approved\": \"Specify an unpredicted terminal minimizer of psi_T+sum_{i<=T}ell_i (then x_(T+1)=x_T in exact-lookahead specialization), or retain the missing terminal prediction/residual terms. Needs independent full derivation/source repair review.\"\n+  },\n+  \"independent_rational_check\": {\n+    \"T\": 1,\n+    \"x1\": \"1/2\",\n+    \"x2_repeat_hint\": \"2/3\",\n+    \"u\": \"1/2\",\n+    \"lambda\": \"2\",\n+    \"initial_stationarity\": \"0\",\n+    \"ghost_stationarity\": \"0\",\n+    \"regret\": \"0\",\n+    \"printed_sharp_rhs\": \"-1/36\",\n+    \"retained_hint_difference\": \"5/72\",\n+    \"unhinted_ghost\": \"1/2\",\n+    \"corrected_BTRL_bound\": \"0\"\n+  },\n+  \"suggested_first_leaf\": \"Finite-value cumulative regret identity with explicit arbitrary-terminal G_T(z)-G_T(u) gap, as already required by repair:7.47-terminal. No optimizer or bound hypothesis.\",\n+  \"source_invalid_scope\": \"Printed sharp inequality with repeated-final-hint quadratic completion; no claim all corrected statements or Young bound refuted.\",\n+  \"remaining_corrected_targets\": [\n+    \"General ghost attainment with psi_(T+1)=psi_T and zero hint\",\n+    \"Finite/extended-value adapters and support/sum-rule qualification\",\n+    \"Strong convex stability and exact dual-norm Young bound\",\n+    \"BTRL terminal xT and corrected t<T movement sum\"\n+  ],\n+  \"proof_started\": false,\n+  \"new_header_frozen\": false,\n+  \"status_changes\": false,\n+  \"report_sha256\": \"bce76c76d847bdaff8ce905d2b73713bc83d5b542377799cab2bd2ac6a8aee05\",\n+  \"inputs_unchanged\": true\n+}"},{"sha":"8d8be234f48a494e75d50e79e22ad22a7d225808","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/next-b4-readiness/v1.md","status":"added","additions":39,"deletions":0,"changes":39,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fv1.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fv1.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fnext-b4-readiness%2Fv1.md?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@\n+# B4 next dependency readiness — proposal only\n+\n+Recommended first theorem-sized leaf: the exact finite-value cumulative-regret identity WITH terminal gap from frozen `repair:7.47-terminal`. It is algebraically ready and requires neither a claimed optimizer nor a fabricated public bound. It must not be labeled the full printed extended-real Lemma7.1 or accepted Algorithm7.8/Theorem7.47. No new header is frozen and no proof is started.\n+\n+## Source invalidity versus corrected target\n+\n+Pinned PDF141–142 (printed129–130) explicitly defines real rounds of Algorithm7.8 with F_t=psi_t+sum_{i<t}ell_i and x_t minimizing F_t+h_t, then receiving and paying ell_t(x_t). No assumptions on how h_t is predicted are supplied. The proof on PDF142 sets h_(T+1)=h_T and psi_(T+1)=psi_T, then cancels the hint telescope. That cancellation omits h_(T+1)(u)-h_(T+1)(z); real rounds are unchanged by the choice of ghost, but the terminal term in the purported bound is not.\n+\n+The retained exact rational T1 witness has V=R, psi=x²/2, ell=h=(x-1)²/2. Actual x1=1/2 minimizes psi+h; the repeated-hint ghost x2=2/3 minimizes psi+2ell. At comparatoru=x1 and lambda1=2, regret0 while the printed sharp RHS=-1/36. Both quadratic minimizers, positivity, smoothness and strong convexity are ordinary exact facts. Independent Fraction arithmetic reconfirms0<=-1/36 is false. Thus SOURCE_INVALID applies to this repeated-hint completion of the sharp inequality, not to every possible repaired endpoint, every real-round algorithm, or automatically the Young bound. The missing terminal hint difference is5/72; retaining it changes the comparison. No blanket source-false claim exceeds this witnessed scope.\n+\n+PDF278–279/printed266–267 gives BTRL actual x_t minimizing G_t=psi_t+sum_{i<=t}ell_i but does not define x_(T+1). Transporting the repeated-last-loss convention gives the same invalid example. Under the existing corrected convention x_(T+1)=x_T, its final movement is zero and the required corrected BTRL sum runs only t<T. This is a labeled source correction, not an equivalent interpretation silently attributed to the printed unspecified terminal.\n+\n+## Exact unchanged next target\n+\n+For T>=1, arbitrary real-valued losses and regularizers on an arbitrary state type, arbitrary x1,...,xT, feasible comparatoru and arbitrary feasible terminal z=x_(T+1), define G_t=psi_t+sum_{i<=t}ell_i. Prove\n+\n+R_T(u)=psi_T(u)-psi1(x1)+sum_{t=1}^T(G_t(x_t)-G_t(x_(t+1)))+sum_{t=1}^{T-1}(psi_t(x_(t+1))-psi_(t+1)(x_(t+1)))+G_T(z)-G_T(u).\n+\n+This is precisely the finite-value algebraic clause already frozen; it neither changes the real-round hints/updates nor assumes a regret bound. No convexity, minimization, probability, concentration theorem or source terminal inequality is needed for the identity. T1 must retain the gap and empty regularizer-change sum. Intended reuse is finite-sum difference/telescoping algebra. A useful subsequent concrete test is the repeated-hint T1 witness showing why deleting the gap is forbidden, plus an unhinted comparator-minimizer specialization. Such tests are suggestions, not new frozen canaries.\n+\n+The theorem is a finite-real specialization. For original EReal losses, a separate explicit finiteness adapter must justify every displayed subtraction before using toReal. Do not obtain the identity by totalizing infinities and call that extended source fidelity. The frozen target expressly requires finite displayed values; any broader extended-value branches remain mandatory and open.\n+\n+## Accepted substrates and bounded availability\n+\n+Current accepted OnlineWeightedPotential RAW and HEAD match its bounded acceptance receipt; weighted_difference_sum_eq is a reusable exact real finite-sum identity, although direct Mathlib sum telescoping may be simpler and no forced dependency is required. Current OnlinePrescientBregmanSource RAW and HEAD match FINAL: sourceProper_of_domain can produce properness from a nonempty finite domain and no-bottom; advance_eq_some_of_minimizer illustrates genuine selected-minimum identification. The proximal selector itself is NOT optimistic FTRL and must not be repurposed by changing its information flow.\n+\n+OnlineFTLSelector exposes strict-past cumulative loss, partial selection and select_eq_some_of_unique; it has no arbitrary time-varying regularizer/hint interface. Its source is inspected and pinned here but separate acceptance is not re-certified. FTRLOneStep consumes an explicit finite-action linear-objective minimizer and provides a one-step inequality: it supplies neither this cumulative identity nor unbounded-domain attainment. TsallisFTRLMinimizerExistence is finite-simplex/half-Tsallis-specific, not a general extended-real strongly-convex attainment theorem. These are availability/reuse-boundary observations, not claims that similarly named modules discharge B4.\n+\n+## Blockers after the algebra leaf\n+\n+1. Preserve Algorithm7.8 actual rounds and all source hypotheses. Introduce only the frozen ghost psi_(T+1)=psi_T,h_(T+1)=0 and prove existence of a feasible minimizer z of G_T under the correctly specified proper/closed/strongly-convex finite-dimensional setting. Do not supply existence as if it were already produced. The difference between proper ambient functions and proper restriction to V must be explicit; merely allowing identically-top feasible objectives cannot justify a finite comparator/ghost.\n+2. Bind the exact intended notion of closedness and strong convexity on V, lambda_t>0, extended indicators and all finite evaluation points. Source h_t is real on V; ell_t-h_t needs its own qualified extended-real/global-support interpretation. An arbitrary ambient extension of a V-only hint cannot silently change the required subdifferential.\n+3. Prove the actual minimizer/support interface: source proof uses0 in partial(F_t+h_t+indicatorV)(x_t) and sum inclusion to insert every permitted ghat in partial(ell_t-h_t)(x_t). Its domain/sum-rule qualification and properness must be justified; no missing public inequality may be substituted as a hypothesis.\n+4. Derive strong-convexity stability at these actual finite points, retain the terminal gap until ghost minimality permits its removal, then derive the dual-norm/Young endpoint with exact1/(2lambda_t). No all-norm conclusion from a Euclidean-only helper without an explicit adapter.\n+5. BTRL corrected bound follows with actual minimizers, terminal xT and t<T movements; optional stronger -lambda_T||u-xT||²/2 remains a separately labeled strengthening. Do not use this optional improvement to omit any required corrected endpoint.\n+\n+No generic OptimisticFTRL/BTRL source producer or general proper-closed-strongly-convex existence terminal was located by the narrow search; this is not an exhaustive theorem-absence proof. For the proposed first finite algebra leaf these analytic issues do not block progress, but they block full B4 closure. All frozen mandatory targets remain OPEN, including necessarydependency ownership under forward:prescient-lookahead. No proof, new headers, root/site/native/status edits, concentration assumptions, or container/Goal completion claim.\n+\n+Source pixels141,142,279 were freshly rendered and personally inspected; PDF278 was read as direct pinned-PDF text in this audit (its algorithm was visually inspected earlier). Input hashes, exact frozen rows, accepted RAW joins and the independent rational calculation are in JSON. Requested Astra/medium metadata; runtime not attested."},{"sha":"bd7ce3e47ec9c65ce702d105765145b877693144","filename":"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/parent-canary-integrity-and-proposal-port-v1.json","status":"added","additions":84,"deletions":0,"changes":84,"blob_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/blob/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fparent-canary-integrity-and-proposal-port-v1.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/raw/2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fparent-canary-integrity-and-proposal-port-v1.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DakeBU/Auto-Bandit-RL-Proof-In-Sleep/contents/runs%2Fonline-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011%2Fparent-canary-integrity-and-proposal-port-v1.json?ref=2615db818eaca2e88b68d94f3b8a58519b65f5c6","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,84 @@\n+{\n+  \"utc\": \"2026-10-11T07:03:54.209521+00:00\",\n+  \"all_canary_manifest_artifacts_exact\": 53,\n+  \"canary_manifest_sha256\": \"00a13313d9d9e18618b2481e75bd4d20eb355cad74ef2536192f03dce9e6558d\",\n+  \"core_and_Test_source_unchanged\": true,\n+  \"ports\": [\n+    {\n+      \"from\": \"tmp/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-integration-proposal-20261011/parent-apply-preflight-v1.json\",\n+      \"to\": \"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/integration-proposal/parent-apply-preflight-v1.json\",\n+      \"sha256\": \"ce1f8174e81a44c8b8dc0266b07048b8c0cf093854f6559e9aef4e550248dd8d\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"from\": \"tmp/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-integration-proposal-20261011/plan-v1.md\",\n+      \"to\": \"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/integration-proposal/plan-v1.md\",\n+      \"sha256\": \"c70ebf9a8de1e2e2bb4c281f76c1c7785fa12bd69e8bc96c636bd48bebb96e5f\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"from\": \"tmp/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-integration-proposal-20261011/plan-v2.md\",\n+      \"to\": \"runs/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-20261011/integration-proposal/plan-v2.md\",\n+      \"sha256\": \"bdc23a493588e942f5cd4e9745ec6c333193e3eddc96684757adaf7b17d55e9b\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"from\": \"tmp/online-ch2-prescient-euclidean-integration-proposal-20261011/proposal-v1